; HetLitmus Layer-1 (Static) cram tests -- see hetlitmus/docs/TEST-PLAN.md sec 4.
; Pure bash/python, no toolchain, no GPU: they exercise the rule functions of
; _grid_lib.sh (basics.t), the oracle-compare.sh decision logic (oracle-
; negatives.t), and ptxcheck.py's discriminating power on a frozen corrupt PTX
; (ptx-negatives.t).  Adapted from TEST-PLAN sec 6: the scripts under test live in
; parent dirs, so they are depended on by their real relative paths (the cram
; sandbox reconstructs the workspace tree and runs with cwd = this dir).  The
; binaries (%{bin:...}) are intentionally omitted -- Layer 1 needs no OCaml build.
(cram
 (applies_to basics oracle-negatives ptx-negatives)
 (deps
  ../_grid_lib.sh
  ../../oracle-compare.sh
  ../../verify/ptxcheck.py
  obs.txt
  oracle.csv
  obs-amd.txt
  oracle-amd.csv
  obs-stats.txt
  oracle-stats.csv
  corrupt-strengthen.ptx
  (glob_files ../gpu-only/*.litmus)))

; pfix-cond.t, coop-launch.t and shared-alloc.t are the exceptions that DO need the
; OCaml build: they emit representative het tests through litmus7 and read the
; generated driver.  pfix-cond guards the Task P condition-compiler fix (7a/7b);
; coop-launch guards the B2 perpetual-loop + cooperative-launch driver shape;
; shared-alloc guards the B1 per-target gd_alloc_shared / gd_free_shared allocator;
; stress guards the B4 GPU memory-stress layer (device-memory scratchpad, pure-stress
; workgroups, and the device-scope window-opener kept SEPARATE from the system-scope
; cross-device rendezvous); cpu-stress guards the B5 CPU + interconnect layer -- the
; host ISA kept out of the nvcc TU, the -2s invariants, the four object classes, and
; above all the stress_go ORDERING, which is the one B5 property the liveness gate
; (hetlitmus/verify/cpustresscheck.py) structurally cannot see: its dynamic probe
; drives het_cpu_stress.h's mechanisms with its own flag and never exercises the
; emitted driver's orchestration.  Confirmed by bite test -- moving the stress_go
; raise after the joins passes cpustresscheck and fails here.
; Scoped so the three Layer-1 tests above stay toolchain-free.  litmus7 het emission
; needs no -set-libdir, so only the binary + the .litmus inputs are required.
; statistics.t guards B7 -- the EMITTER half of the statistics layer.  The estimator's
; own behaviour is gated by hetlitmus/verify/statscheck.py (which compiles the real
; het_verdict.h and drives it with synthetic record streams); what cram pins is that the
; harness actually PRODUCES the inputs that layer consumes -- above all the per-window
; sub-tallies, and the fact that the count and its window bump ride ONE line under ONE
; predicate, which is what makes sum(win[]) == total an invariant the run can check.
(cram
 (applies_to pfix-cond coop-launch shared-alloc tagged-recover stress cpu-stress
   positive-control statistics)
 (package herdtools7)
 (deps
  %{bin:litmus7}
  ; B6b: emitting a should-be-FORBIDDEN test now READS two more corpus tests --
  ; mu(T) and the canary, named by the derived control map -- because the harness
  ; CO-RUNS them.  Naming each one here would silently break the moment a cram
  ; test touched a different Disallowed row (the emitter would refuse, and the
  ; guard would fail with "no such file" rather than on its own subject), so the
  ; whole corpus is staged.  The corpus IS the mutation lattice; the controls are
  ; drawn from it, and there is no principled subset.
  (glob_files ../het/*.litmus)
  ; The map itself is an INPUT to emission, not just a doc: it is what names the
  ; controls, and it is DERIVED from the corpus + the oracle (controlmap.py), never
  ; rewritten from the test's name.
  ../het/control-map.csv
  ../gpu-only/MP-sys-acquire.litmus))

; gpu-target.t is separate because it is the only cram test that emits a PLAIN
; CPU-only test -- the boundary check that `-gpu-target' did not become a
; condition on upstream litmus7 usage.  That path needs litmus7's libdir, and the
; libdir cannot be a declared dep either: its per-arch subdirectories are named
; `_aarch64', `_x86_64', ... and dune ignores directories starting with `_', so
; (source_tree ../../../litmus/libdir) stages the top level and NONE of the arch
; files -- silently, exactly the trap amd-cat.t documents for herd/libdir.  So
; this test walks up from cwd to the source libdir, the same way, and the stanza
; above stays as it was.
(cram
 (applies_to gpu-target)
 (package herdtools7)
 (deps
  %{bin:litmus7}
  (glob_files ../het/*.litmus)
  ../het/control-map.csv
  ../gpu-only/MP-sys-acquire.litmus))

; amd-cat.t is the third stanza because it is the only cram test that runs herd7.
; It guards hetlitmus/cats/amd-gcn3.cat after the PORT2-R2 D14 repair
; (SC-per-location, the 'sc set, the two fence-ordering mechanisms, and the
; tag2events fence seam).  That file's own validation -- "8/8 against the PLDI'23
; artifact" -- is provably blind to every one of those: the test measures that all
; eight of its ablations still score 8/8.  The 137-test GPU-only corpus is blind to
; the coherence half as well -- three of the eight ablations (A0, A1, A01) move 0 of
; its 137 rows.  For those the probes are the ONLY instrument in the tree that sees
; the repair, which is why D14 asked for a cram test and not a corpus count.
;
; herd7's libdir is NOT and CANNOT BE a declared dep: herd/dune line 1 reads
; `(dirs :standard \ tests/instructions libdir)`, so herd/libdir is outside dune's
; source scan altogether and both (source_tree ...) and (glob_files ...) on it
; resolve to NOTHING -- silently, which is worse than an error.  (Measured: with
; either form declared, every herd7 invocation below still died with "Cannot find
; file stdlib.cat".)  litmus7 does not have this problem because het emission needs
; no libdir at all.  amd-cat.t therefore locates the SOURCE libdir at run time by
; walking up from cwd until it finds herd/libdir/stdlib.cat, which works from the
; build dir and from a dune sandbox alike.  Consequence, stated so nobody assumes
; otherwise: editing a file under herd/libdir does NOT invalidate this test's dune
; cache.  Run `make hetlitmus-cram` after touching herd/libdir, or `dune build
; @hetlitmus/tests/cram/amd-cat --force`.  If the walk-up ever fails the test does
; not go quiet -- herd7 prints its libdir error, every verdict line comes out empty
; and the golden mismatches on the first probe.
(cram
 (applies_to amd-cat)
 (package herdtools7)
 (deps
  %{bin:herd7}
  ../../bells/ptx.bell
  ../../cats/amd-gcn3.cat
  (glob_files ../../cats/probes/*)
  (glob_files ../gpu-only/*.litmus)))
